THE STR ROWS ARE THE LOAD-BEARING CONTROL. They run through the SAME function
this edit changed, just the other branch of the same gate, and they are flat:
MultiGraph 0.4983/0.4925 -> 0.5065/0.4936, MultiDiGraph 0.4837/0.4742 ->
0.4929/0.4857. That is what shows the widening did not disturb the path that was
already fast.

ONE CONTROL DID MOVE AND IT IS NOT EXPLAINED BY THIS CHANGE. DiGraph int keys
went 0.5244/0.5031 -> 0.5805/0.5405, a one-sided ~9% rise on a class this hunk
cannot reach (PyDiGraph::get_edge_data is a different impl block). The Graph
rows and both str rows are flat, so this is not a systematic arm difference.
Read it as the LTO binary noise floor - adding code to lib.rs and digraph.rs
changes inlining decisions across the crate, and identical-command builds
differing in one function are already documented to disagree by ~5% on untouched
rows. 9% is above that but the same family. It is recorded rather than smoothed
over; it does not touch the target conclusion, where the effect is ~100% against
a ~9% control wobble.

Also note the key-type penalty INVERTS to match the simple classes: MultiGraph
raw native int/str went 2.26x -> 0.93x, i.e. int is now marginally faster than
str, exactly as Graph (0.97x) and DiGraph (0.98x) already were.

STILL A LOSS. 0.53-0.60x is not parity and is not claimed as such. What this
closes is the key-type asymmetry; the remaining gap is the multigraph keyed
lookup's own cost against networkx's two chained dict subscripts.

PARITY. The int-key sweep the bead names as a landing gate
(tests/artifacts/perf/20260825T-intkey-canonical-blackthrush/parity_intkeys.py)
covers Graph and DiGraph ONLY - not the classes this touches. I wrote the
missing coverage: 792 checks over keyed and unkeyed lookups, parallel edges,
explicit int and str edge keys, reversed endpoints, i64 boundaries, ints past
i64, int subclasses, bool, the 1/1.0/True collapse, 5 vs "5" staying distinct,
and five read/mutate/read staleness sequences (add_parallel, remove_edge,
remove_node, add_node, clear_edge) plus repeated-read identity and write-through
visibility. 0 failures on BOTH arms, and the shipped sweep also passes on both.

The staleness sequences are the ones that matter here: widening the probe means
an int-keyed read now HITS a lookaside it previously could never consult, so
every staleness bug in that cache became reachable for the first time. The
comparator carries a self-test proving it can actually fail.

